@prefix owl:      <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix adms:     <http://www.w3.org/ns/adms#> .
@prefix dcterms:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix cc:       <http://creativecommons.org/ns#> .
@prefix xsd:      <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs:     <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix rdf:      <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix skos:     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix vann:     <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/> .
@prefix arch:     <https://meta.linked.archi/core#> .
@prefix arch-vis: <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#> .
@prefix schema:   <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix :         <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#> .

<https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#>
    rdf:type        owl:Ontology ;
    cc:license      <http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/> ;
    cc:license      "This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported License."@en ;
    vann:preferredNamespaceUri    "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#" ;
    vann:preferredNamespacePrefix "arch-vis" ;
    dcterms:creator      "Kalin Maldzhanski"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:description  '''Core Linked.Archi Ontology for Diagrams and Visual Notation.
Provides vocabulary for diagram interchange (nodes, links, bounds) and visual styling
(colors, fonts, shapes, icons). Used both at the instance level (diagram elements) and
at the type level (default notation for model concepts via NotationSet).'''@en ;
    dcterms:issued       "2019-03-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:modified     "2026-11-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:publisher    <https://linked.archi> ;
    dcterms:publisher    "Linked.Archi"@en ;
    dcterms:title        "Core Linked.Archi Ontology for Diagrams and Visual Notation"@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.9"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis-shapes#> ;
    skos:historyNote '''0.9: A diagram may hold a reference to another view. arch-vis:ViewReferenceNode is
the appearance of a view on another diagram, as an ArchNode is an element's: a node that opens the view
its arch-vis:referencesView names, such as an index view's entry for each view it lists, or the way from
an element's box to the view that details it. The ArchiMate exchange format writes one as a Label node
with a viewRef. It is not a LabelNode, which stands for nothing:
it stands for a view. arch-vis:referencesView pairs with it as arch-vis:archElement does with ArchNode,
its rdfs:domain the class, and functional. It is not arch-vis:view, the view a diagram element is drawn
in.
0.8: A model element's picture for the presentation context shown (core's
arch:prefVisNotation, arch:altVisNotation) is drawn where its diagram element is drawn as a box too —
arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge or arch-vis:ShapeOnly — inside it, at the diagram element's
arch-vis:imagePosition, unless its own style gives it a picture of its own (arch-vis:image) or an icon
of its own (arch-vis:iconSymbol); the badge stays the notation's glyph. Where 0.7 drew it only as the
icon of a diagram element drawn arch-vis:IconCentric, choosing an audience showed nothing of it on a
box. And a style may say arch-vis:useElementPicture: the diagram element's icon is its model element's
picture for the context shown — its badge, drawn arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge, or its figure, drawn
IconCentric — in place of the notation's glyph, following the picture as it changes and as the context
does.

0.7: A diagram element's own style says more than its colours and its font:
arch-vis:lineWidth, the width of its outline or its line; arch-vis:fontStyle, whether its text is
bold, italic or underlined; where its text sits — arch-vis:textAlignment across it
(arch-vis:AlignLeft, AlignCenter, AlignRight), arch-vis:textPosition down it
(arch-vis:PositionTop, PositionMiddle, PositionBottom); arch-vis:gradient, the side its fill shades
from (arch-vis:GradientTop, GradientLeft, GradientRight, GradientBottom); arch-vis:iconColor,
its glyph's colour; arch-vis:image, a picture drawn in it, at arch-vis:imagePosition — an
arch-vis:IconPlacement, which gains MiddleLeft, MiddleRight, BottomLeft, BottomRight and Fill; and,
for a line, arch-vis:labelAnchor, where along it its text sits (arch-vis:AnchorSource, AnchorMiddle,
AnchorTarget). Each absent leaves the choice to the notation or the renderer. A label may say more than
a name: arch-vis:labelExpression gives an arch-vis:LabelExpression, its arch-vis:expression text with
{…} placeholders for the concept's name, its type and its properties — on a diagram element's own style,
on a concept's notation, or on a presentation context for everything shown in it — and, with core's
arch:inPresentationContext (core 0.5.0), for one presentation context only, so that each audience may
read the same element its own way. So may it look its own way: arch-vis:style is no longer functional —
a diagram element has at most one style for every context and one for each context, the latter drawn
over the former in its context — and a diagram element drawn arch-vis:IconCentric shows its model
element's picture for the context (core's arch:prefVisNotation, arch:altVisNotation) where its own style
names no icon. arch-vis:lineStyle, sourceDecor and targetDecor
name what they may be said of with arch:domainIncludes — a relationship, a relationship type's
notation, a style, and so one line's own — where an rdfs:domain of arch:QualifiedRelationship made
every notation saying them a relationship. arch-vis:alternativeStyle gives a notation other figures for
its concept — ArchiMate's banded, tabbed, wavy and 3D boxes, its cloud and folder, now that each element
is drawn by default as a box with its glyph. A colour's alpha is CSS's, 0 to 1: the comments
had given an example with the exchange format's percentage, rgba(255,255,181,100), which CSS reads
as opaque whatever it is.

0.6: A Link may end on another Link. A relationship can have a relationship as
its arch:source or arch:target, and its edge on a diagram then ends on the edge drawn for that
relationship — as a connection may end on a connection in the ArchiMate exchange format. The
range of arch-vis:source and arch-vis:target is widened from arch-vis:Node to
arch-vis:DiagElement; core-vis-shapes restricts both to a Node or a Link, since a Point is
never an end.
0.5: Nesting. arch-vis:inNode places a node inside another on the same
diagram — a grouping around its members, a node around the system software it hosts. And
arch-vis:nestedEnd says, on a relationship's notation, that nesting may draw the relationship
and which end goes inside the other (arch-vis:SourceEnd, arch-vis:TargetEnd): an editor that
nests one element in another can then offer the relationships the nesting may stand for.

0.4: A notation declares the shapes it needs beyond the core ones in its own
namespace, with arch-vis:fallbackShape (a core shape for renderers that cannot draw it) and its
geometry as path templates: arch-vis:shapeVariables, arch-vis:outlinePath, arch-vis:detailPath,
arch-vis:contentBox and arch-vis:labelBox. The ArchiMate, EDGY and C4 notations declare theirs
that way. Added arch-vis:borderStyle (a Style's outline LineStyle) for
dashed outlines such as ArchiMate's grouping. Documented arch-vis:fontColor as the label colour,
the line colour when absent. Documented the arch-vis:decorSymbol drawing
convention, and that an arch-vis:iconSymbol glyph is transparent where the element's fill shows
through. arch-vis:CircleDot is a filled dot, as ArchiMate's assignment draws it.

0.3: Widened arch-vis:label's domain from arch-vis:Node to
arch-vis:DiagElement — an edge's label is the ordinary case (BPMN BPMNEdge-label, a named
sequence flow). Declared Node, Link and Point mutually disjoint (arch-vis:NodeLinkPointDisjoint),
which makes a wrong domain detectable rather than silently type-adding. Companion SHACL shapes
published as core-vis-shapes.

0.2: Extended Style with structural properties (shapeType, iconSymbol,
iconPlacement, defaultWidth, defaultHeight). Added ShapeType, IconPlacement, LineStyle,
and Decoration individuals. Added NotationSet and VisualNotation classes for type-level
visual notation management. Added relationship line properties (lineStyle, sourceDecor,
targetDecor) on QualifiedRelationship.'''@en ;
.
# Common classes and properties for visual modeling and diagrams

arch-vis:DiagElement
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "DiagElement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Anything that appears on a diagram — a shape, an edge or a routing point.
The common superclass of Node, Link and Point, and the domain of the properties that
apply to all three (arch-vis:view, arch-vis:label, styling).'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:Node
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:label      "Node"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    "A shape on a diagram: it has bounds and may contain other nodes."@en ;
.

arch-vis:ContainerNode
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:label      "Container"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    '''A shape on a diagram that stands for no model element: a group around what is
drawn inside it (arch-vis:inNode), a pool, a lane — or a plain shape, a rectangle, an ellipse, a
diamond, whose own arch-vis:style gives its arch-vis:shapeType. It carries no arch-vis:archElement.
Its text, when it has one (a group's name), is its skos:prefLabel, language-tagged; its colours
are its style's (arch-vis:fillColor, arch-vis:lineColor, arch-vis:fontColor). The ArchiMate
exchange format's Container node is one.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:LabelNode
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:label      "LabelNode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    '''Text on a diagram. Pointed at by another diagram element's arch-vis:label, it
places that element's text apart from it — a relationship's name moved off the middle of its line —
and the text is the name of what that element draws, unless the LabelNode gives its own as
skos:prefLabel. Pointed at by none, it is free-standing text: a note, its text its skos:prefLabel,
language-tagged, its colours its arch-vis:style's. The ArchiMate exchange format's Label node is
one. Either way it stands for no model element and carries no arch-vis:archElement. A Label node
with a viewRef is not one: it stands for the view it opens, an arch-vis:ViewReferenceNode.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:ArchNode
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:label      "ArchNode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    '''The appearance of a model element on a diagram: its arch-vis:archElement names
the element. An element may be drawn by more than one ArchNode in one view — placed again to keep
lines short — each a node of its own, moved, nested and removed on its own.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:ViewReferenceNode
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:label      "ViewReferenceNode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    '''The appearance of a view on another diagram, as an ArchNode is an element's: a node
that opens the view its arch-vis:referencesView names, exactly one. An index view holds one for each
view it lists; an element's box may hold one for the view that details it, drawn inside it
(arch-vis:inNode). Its text is its skos:prefLabel, language-tagged, its colours its arch-vis:style's.
It is not a LabelNode: a note stands for nothing, and this stands for a view. It carries no
arch-vis:archElement, a view being no arch:Element. The ArchiMate exchange format writes one as a Label
node with a viewRef.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:Link
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:label      "Link"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    '''An edge on a diagram: it joins a source to a target, may route through a
PointList, and may carry a LabelNode. Each end is a Node, or another Link where the relationship
the edge draws has a relationship as its source or target. A relationship may be drawn by more
than one Link in one view, each joining the nodes it is drawn between.

A Link with no arch-vis:archRelationship stands for no relationship: a line drawn on the diagram
only — from a note to what it is about, as the ArchiMate exchange format's Line connection — its
ends Nodes. It puts nothing in the view's arch:inView.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:Point
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:label      "Point"@en ;
    rdfs:comment    "A single x/y waypoint on a Link's route."@en ;
.

#################################################################
# Disjointness
#
# Node, Link and Point partition DiagElement. Without these axioms
# nothing says a Node is not a Link, so a too-narrow rdfs:domain on a
# property asserted about an edge silently adds a type instead of being
# reported. The axiom is what makes arch-vis:label's DiagElement domain
# a real constraint rather than a comment.
#
# Declared as a single owl:AllDisjointClasses rather than pairwise
# owl:disjointWith so the partition is one resource to read and to extend.
#################################################################

arch-vis:NodeLinkPointDisjoint
    a                 owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    rdfs:label        "Node / Link / Point are mutually disjoint"@en ;
    owl:members       ( arch-vis:Node arch-vis:Link arch-vis:Point ) ;
.

arch-vis:PointList
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment    "List of points"@en ;
    rdfs:label      "List of points"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a                  owl:Class ;
                      owl:intersectionOf ( rdf:List [ a                 owl:Restriction ;
                                                      owl:allValuesFrom arch-vis:Point ;
                                                      owl:onProperty    rdf:first ] [ a              owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                      owl:allValuesFrom
                                                                                                     arch-vis:PointList ;
                                                                                      owl:onProperty rdf:rest ] ) ] .

arch-vis:bounds-x
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.


arch-vis:bounds-y
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:bounds-w
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.


arch-vis:bounds-h
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:point-x
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Point ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:point-y
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   ""@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Point ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:points
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "points"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The ordered list of points of a link's route, in the diagram's coordinates. The
first and last are its ends — where it meets its nodes — when they lie on their nodes' outlines;
otherwise they are bends, and the line runs from each node to them, meeting the node's outline
where a line laid out without them would. A route of bends alone is how the ArchiMate exchange
format has it; a route with its ends, how BPMN DI has it. One point is one bend.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Link ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:PointList ;
.

arch-vis:label
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "label"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The LabelNode carrying this diagram element's rendered text.
Domain is DiagElement, not Node: both shapes and edges are labellable. BPMN declares
BPMNShape-label and BPMNEdge-label as separate composite properties, and a named
sequence flow — an edge with a label — is the ordinary case, not an edge case. Narrowing
the domain to Node would entail, through the domain axiom, that every labelled edge is
also a Node, which arch-vis:NodeLinkPointDisjoint now reports as an inconsistency.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:LabelNode
.

arch-vis:archElement
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "archElement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment ""@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ArchNode ;
    rdfs:range   arch:Element ;
.


arch-vis:archRelationship
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "archRelationship"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Links a diagram Link to the qualified relationship resource it represents.
Absent, the Link stands for no relationship: a line drawn on the diagram only.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Link ;
    rdfs:range   arch:QualifiedRelationship ;
.

arch-vis:referencesView
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "referencesView"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The view a ViewReferenceNode stands for and opens, as arch-vis:archElement names the
element an ArchNode stands for. Not the view the node is drawn in, which is its arch-vis:view. The
ArchiMate exchange format allows a viewRef, a drill-down, on any node or connection; only a Label
node's makes a ViewReferenceNode, and a drill-down from an element's box or from a line is not this
property's.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ViewReferenceNode ;
    rdfs:range   arch:View ;
.

arch-vis:source
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The diagram element a Link starts from: a Node, or another Link where the
relationship the Link draws starts at a relationship.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Link ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:DiagElement ;
.

arch-vis:target
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "target"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The diagram element a Link ends at: a Node, or another Link where the
relationship the Link draws ends at a relationship.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Link ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:DiagElement ;
.

#TODO link all elements to arch:View ... or use quads ?

arch-vis:view
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "view"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The arch:View this diagram element belongs to. Every DiagElement has exactly one."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:range   arch:View ;
.

arch-vis:inNode
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "inNode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The node this one is drawn inside, on the same diagram: a container. Moving the
container moves what is in it, and it stays large enough to hold it. Stated by the nested
node, as membership is (arch:inView). Bounds stay in the diagram's coordinates, not the
container's. Nesting is drawing: what it means in the model, if anything, is a relationship of
its own (see arch-vis:nestedEnd).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Node ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Node ;
.

# Style

arch-vis:Style
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "Style"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Visual style applied to a diagram element (fill, line, font)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:style
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "style"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''How a diagram element looks. It has at most one style for every presentation context — one
without core's arch:inPresentationContext — and at most one for each context. Drawn for a context, it
takes its style for every context, overlaid value by value by its style for that context; drawn for none,
its style for every context alone. A diagram element with one style looks the same to everyone.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:DiagElement ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Style ;
.

arch-vis:fillColor
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fillColor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''A CSS colour string — #rrggbb, #rrggbbaa, rgb() or rgba(), e.g. #fff7b3, or
rgba(255,247,179,0.5) half transparent. Its alpha is CSS's, from 0 (transparent) to 1 (opaque); opaque
when it has none. Converters before core-vis 0.7 wrote the ArchiMate exchange format's alpha as it is,
a percentage (rgba(255,247,179,100)); a reader may take an alpha above 1 as one.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:lineColor
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "lineColor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A CSS colour string, its alpha CSS's (0–1), as arch-vis:fillColor's."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:fontName
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fontName"@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:fontSize
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fontSize"@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:integer ;
.

arch-vis:fontColor
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fontColor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Colour of the element's label, as a CSS colour string (its alpha CSS's, as
arch-vis:fillColor's). When it is absent a renderer draws the label in the line colour
(arch-vis:lineColor).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:gradient
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "gradient"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The fill shades from its colour (arch-vis:fillColor) at the side named
(arch-vis:GradientTop, GradientLeft, GradientRight, GradientBottom) to white at the opposite side.
Absent, the fill is all one colour.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Gradient ;
.

arch-vis:iconColor
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "iconColor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Colour of the glyph drawn for an element (arch-vis:iconSymbol) — its badge, or its icon —
as a CSS colour string: the glyph's opaque lines are drawn in it. Absent, the glyph is drawn as
published.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:fontStyle
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fontStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''How the text is set: one or more of the keywords bold, italic and underline,
separated by spaces ("bold", "bold italic"), as the ArchiMate exchange format's font style lists
them. Plain when absent.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:lineWidth
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "lineWidth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Width of the lines drawn — a node's outline, a link's line — in the diagram's
units, as its bounds are. 1 when absent; 1, 2 and 3 are a normal, medium and heavy line, the values
the exchange format's style lineWidth carries.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:integer ;
.

arch-vis:textAlignment
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "textAlignment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where a node's text sits across it: from its left, centred, or to its right.
Absent, the renderer's choice — centred, for an element.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:TextAlignment ;
.

arch-vis:textPosition
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "textPosition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where a node's text sits down it: from its top, in its middle, or at its bottom.
Absent, the renderer's choice.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:TextPosition ;
.

arch-vis:labelAnchor
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "labelAnchor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where along a line its text sits — a relationship's name, or what a line standing for no
relationship says: near its source end, at its middle, or near its target end. Of a relationship type's
lines, said on its notation or its notation's default style; of one line, on that Link's own style
(arch-vis:style). Absent, at the middle. Where the Link has an arch-vis:label — its text moved by hand
— the text is drawn there.'''@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes arch-vis:VisualNotation, arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:LabelAnchor ;
.


#################################################################
# Extended Style Properties (structural)
#################################################################

arch-vis:shapeType
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "shapeType"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The geometric shape used to render the element (e.g., rounded rectangle, hexagon, circle)."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:ShapeType ;
.

arch-vis:iconSymbol
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "iconSymbol"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''URI of the small glyph/icon displayed within the element shape (typically top-right corner).
The glyph is drawn over the shape as it is: its lines are opaque and the parts that show the element's
fill are transparent, so one glyph serves every fill colour and notation set. On a diagram element's own
style (arch-vis:style) it is that element's own icon, in place of its notation's glyph: a picture drawn
as the figure itself with arch-vis:IconCentric, or as its badge with arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge. A diagram
element drawn arch-vis:IconCentric with no icon of its own shows its model element's picture for the
presentation context shown, when it has one (see arch-vis:IconCentric); one whose style says
arch-vis:useElementPicture shows it as its badge too.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:useElementPicture
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "useElementPicture"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''On a diagram element's own style (arch-vis:style), true: its icon is its model element's
picture for the presentation context shown — core's arch:altVisNotation with that
arch:inPresentationContext, else its arch:prefVisNotation — in place of its notation's glyph: its badge,
drawn arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge, or its figure, drawn arch-vis:IconCentric. The picture is followed, not
copied: a new picture, or another context chosen, is drawn as it is. An arch-vis:iconSymbol of its own
comes first; with no picture for the element, the notation's glyph. Where it is absent, a diagram element
drawn IconCentric with no icon of its own shows the picture as its figure all the same, and one drawn as
a box shows it inside (see arch-vis:IconCentric).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:boolean ;
    skos:example '''A diagram element whose badge, for executives, is its element's picture for them:

  ex:node-1-style-exec a arch-vis:Style ;
      arch:inPresentationContext ctx:Executive ;
      arch-vis:renderingMode arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge ;
      arch-vis:useElementPicture true .'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:iconPlacement
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "iconPlacement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the icon symbol is placed within the element shape."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
.

arch-vis:image
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "image"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''URI of a picture drawn inside a node, at arch-vis:imagePosition: over its fill, under its
text and its glyph. An http(s) URL, or a data: URI of an image for a small picture kept in the
model. The picture is drawn at its own size, made smaller to fit the node; at arch-vis:Fill, scaled
to the node's bounds. The node's glyph is drawn as its rendering mode says. A picture standing for the
figure itself is the node's own arch-vis:iconSymbol, drawn arch-vis:IconCentric. A node drawn as a box
with no image of its own, and no icon of its own, shows its model element's picture for the presentation
context shown here — at its arch-vis:imagePosition — when it has one (see arch-vis:IconCentric).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:imagePosition
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "imagePosition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where a node's picture (arch-vis:image) is drawn in it: a corner, the middle of a side, its
centre, or arch-vis:Fill, the whole node. Absent, its top left.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
.

arch-vis:defaultWidth
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "defaultWidth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Default width in logical units for palette rendering."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:defaultHeight
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "defaultHeight"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Default height in logical units for palette rendering."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:double ;
.

arch-vis:renderingMode
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "renderingMode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''How the element is visually composed from its shape and icon.
Determines the rendering algorithm a tool should use:
- ShapeWithBadge: colored shape with a small icon badge (ArchiMate standard)
- IconCentric: the icon IS the element, label rendered below (AWS/Azure/GCP style)
- ShapeOnly: colored shape with label, no icon (simplified notation)'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:RenderingMode ;
.

arch-vis:borderStyle
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "borderStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The line style of the shape's outline: solid when absent. ArchiMate's grouping is dashed."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:LineStyle ;
.


#################################################################
# ShapeType Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:ShapeType
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "ShapeType"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Geometric shapes used for element rendering. The individuals below are drawn by every
renderer; a notation declares any other shape it needs in its own namespace, with a fallback and
its geometry (see arch-vis:fallbackShape).'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:RoundedRectangle
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Rounded Rectangle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rectangle with rounded corners — the default ArchiMate element shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Rectangle
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Rectangle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sharp-cornered rectangle."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Octagon
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Octagon"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Eight-sided polygon — used for motivation elements in some notations."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Ellipse
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Ellipse"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Oval/ellipse shape — used for values, junctions."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Circle
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Circle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Perfect circle — used for junctions and connectors."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Hexagon
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Hexagon"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Six-sided polygon — used for events in some notations."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Diamond
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Diamond"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rotated square — used for decision points."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Parallelogram
    a            arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:label   "Parallelogram"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Slanted rectangle — used for interfaces in some notations."@en ;
.

# A notation's own shapes
#
# The individuals above are the shapes every renderer draws natively. A notation
# that needs another shape declares it in its own notation file: an
# arch-vis:ShapeType individual in its namespace with an arch-vis:fallbackShape
# (one of the shapes above, drawn by a renderer that cannot draw this one) and
# its geometry as path templates, so any renderer can draw it from the data.
#
# A template is SVG path data in absolute commands (M L H V C S Q T A Z), in the
# shape's own coordinates — (0,0) top left, (w,h) bottom right — where any number
# may be written as {expression}. An expression uses numbers, w, h and the
# shape's variables, + - * / and parentheses, and the functions min() and max().
# Example: M0,{d} L{d},0 L{w},0 with the variable d = min(h * 0.2, 12).

arch-vis:fallbackShape
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "fallbackShape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The core shape (RoundedRectangle, Rectangle, Octagon, Ellipse, Circle, Hexagon, Diamond or
Parallelogram) a renderer draws in place of a notation's own shape when it cannot draw that one.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:ShapeType ;
.

arch-vis:shapeVariables
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "shapeVariables"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Named values a shape's templates use, as assignments separated by semicolons and evaluated
in order, each able to use the ones before it: "b = min(h * 0.173, 14); t = 2 * b".'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:outlinePath
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "outlinePath"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The shape's outline, as a path template: filled with the element's fill colour, stroked with
its line colour, and what connectors meet. It may have several closed parts (a person's head and
body).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:detailPath
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "detailPath"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Lines drawn over the outline, as a path template — a band, a tab, a 3D box's front edges, a
cylinder's rim: stroked with the line colour, not filled, and not part of the outline connectors
meet.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:contentBox
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "contentBox"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where the badge and the label go: "x y width height", each a number or an {expression}
(below a band, on a 3D box's front face). The whole shape when absent.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:labelBox
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "labelBox"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Where the label goes when that is not the content box, as "x y width height": a folder's
name in its tab. The label takes as many lines as the box is tall enough for.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:ShapeType ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.



#################################################################
# IconPlacement Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:IconPlacement
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "IconPlacement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Enumeration of positions within a shape: where its icon is placed (arch-vis:iconPlacement),
or its picture (arch-vis:imagePosition).'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:TopRight
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Top Right"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Icon placed in the top-right corner of the shape (ArchiMate default)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:TopLeft
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Top Left"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Icon placed in the top-left corner of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:TopCenter
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Top Center"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Icon placed at the top center of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Center
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Center"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Icon placed at the center of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:BottomCenter
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Bottom Center"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Icon placed at the bottom center of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:MiddleLeft
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Middle Left"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Placed at the middle of the shape's left side."@en ;
.

arch-vis:MiddleRight
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Middle Right"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Placed at the middle of the shape's right side."@en ;
.

arch-vis:BottomLeft
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Bottom Left"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Placed in the bottom-left corner of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:BottomRight
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Bottom Right"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Placed in the bottom-right corner of the shape."@en ;
.

arch-vis:Fill
    a            arch-vis:IconPlacement ;
    rdfs:label   "Fill"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scaled to the shape's bounds, filling it: a node's picture (arch-vis:imagePosition)."@en ;
.


#################################################################
# Gradient Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:Gradient
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "Gradient"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of the sides a fill shades from (arch-vis:gradient)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:GradientTop
    a            arch-vis:Gradient ;
    rdfs:label   "Gradient Top"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The fill colour at the top, white at the bottom."@en ;
.

arch-vis:GradientLeft
    a            arch-vis:Gradient ;
    rdfs:label   "Gradient Left"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The fill colour at the left, white at the right."@en ;
.

arch-vis:GradientRight
    a            arch-vis:Gradient ;
    rdfs:label   "Gradient Right"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The fill colour at the right, white at the left."@en ;
.

arch-vis:GradientBottom
    a            arch-vis:Gradient ;
    rdfs:label   "Gradient Bottom"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The fill colour at the bottom, white at the top."@en ;
.


#################################################################
# TextAlignment and TextPosition Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:TextAlignment
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "TextAlignment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of where a node's text sits across it (arch-vis:textAlignment)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AlignLeft
    a            arch-vis:TextAlignment ;
    rdfs:label   "Align Left"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Each line of the text starts at the node's left."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AlignCenter
    a            arch-vis:TextAlignment ;
    rdfs:label   "Align Center"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Each line of the text is centred across the node."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AlignRight
    a            arch-vis:TextAlignment ;
    rdfs:label   "Align Right"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Each line of the text ends at the node's right."@en ;
.

arch-vis:TextPosition
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "TextPosition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of where a node's text sits down it (arch-vis:textPosition)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:PositionTop
    a            arch-vis:TextPosition ;
    rdfs:label   "Position Top"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text starts at the node's top."@en ;
.

arch-vis:PositionMiddle
    a            arch-vis:TextPosition ;
    rdfs:label   "Position Middle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text is centred down the node."@en ;
.

arch-vis:PositionBottom
    a            arch-vis:TextPosition ;
    rdfs:label   "Position Bottom"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text ends at the node's bottom."@en ;
.


#################################################################
# LabelAnchor Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:LabelAnchor
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "LabelAnchor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of where along a line its text sits (arch-vis:labelAnchor)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AnchorSource
    a            arch-vis:LabelAnchor ;
    rdfs:label   "Anchor Source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text sits on the line near its source end."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AnchorMiddle
    a            arch-vis:LabelAnchor ;
    rdfs:label   "Anchor Middle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text sits at the middle of the line."@en ;
.

arch-vis:AnchorTarget
    a            arch-vis:LabelAnchor ;
    rdfs:label   "Anchor Target"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The text sits on the line near its target end."@en ;
.


#################################################################
# RenderingMode Individuals
#################################################################

arch-vis:RenderingMode
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "RenderingMode"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rendering Mode"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Determines how a tool visually composes an element from its shape and icon. Each mode specifies a different rendering algorithm.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote '''A tool implementing element rendering MUST select the algorithm based on the renderingMode value on the Style.

## Algorithm by Mode

### ShapeWithBadge (default)
1. Draw shape (shapeType) at defaultWidth × defaultHeight
2. Fill with fillColor, stroke with lineColor
3. Center the label (skos:prefLabel) in the shape
4. If iconSymbol present: render as small badge (16-24px) at iconPlacement position
5. If iconSymbol absent: render as ShapeOnly (graceful degradation)

### IconCentric
1. Render iconSymbol at full size (defaultWidth × defaultHeight), centered
2. Draw minimal container/border (optional, for selection state) using lineColor
3. Render label BELOW the icon, not inside a shape
4. fillColor applies to container background only (often transparent)
5. Requires iconSymbol — if absent, fall back to ShapeOnly with warning

### ShapeOnly
1. Draw shape (shapeType) at defaultWidth × defaultHeight
2. Fill with fillColor, stroke with lineColor
3. Center the label in the shape
4. Ignore iconSymbol and iconPlacement entirely

## Fallback Behavior
- renderingMode absent → default to ShapeWithBadge
- ShapeWithBadge without iconSymbol → render as ShapeOnly
- IconCentric without iconSymbol → log warning, fall back to ShapeOnly
- iconPlacement absent but iconSymbol present → default to TopRight'''@en ;
    rdfs:comment "How the element is visually composed from its shape and icon. See skos:scopeNote for the rendering algorithm."@en ;
.

arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge
    a            arch-vis:RenderingMode ;
    rdfs:label   "Shape with Badge"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shape with Badge"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Colored geometric shape with a small icon badge in one corner and the element label centered. The shape and fill color carry the primary visual identity; the icon is a secondary classifier.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote '''Algorithm:
1. Draw shape (shapeType) at defaultWidth × defaultHeight
2. Fill with fillColor, stroke with lineColor
3. Center the label (skos:prefLabel) in the shape
4. Render iconSymbol as a small badge (16-24px) at iconPlacement position
5. If no iconSymbol → render as ShapeOnly (no badge)

Visual layout:
┌──────────────────────┐
│                  [⚙] │  ← iconSymbol at iconPlacement (e.g., TopRight)
│                      │
│    Element Label     │  ← skos:prefLabel, centered
│                      │
└──────────────────────┘
     filled with fillColor

Used by: ArchiMate, BPMN, UML standard notations.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:IconCentric
    a            arch-vis:RenderingMode ;
    rdfs:label   "Icon-Centric"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Icon-Centric"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The icon IS the element — rendered at full size with the label below. Used for vendor icon sets (AWS, Azure, GCP, Kubernetes) where the icon itself is the primary visual identity.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote '''Algorithm:
1. Render iconSymbol at full size (defaultWidth × defaultHeight) centered in bounds
2. Draw minimal container/border (optional, for selection/hover state) using lineColor
3. Render label (skos:prefLabel) BELOW the icon, not inside a shape
4. fillColor applies to container background only (often transparent/white)
5. iconPlacement MUST be Center for this mode

Visual layout:
      ┌────────┐
      │  [☁️]  │  ← iconSymbol rendered at full size
      └────────┘
    Element Label     ← label below, not inside shape

Requires: iconSymbol MUST be present. If absent, fall back to ShapeOnly with warning.
Used by: AWS Architecture Icons, Azure service icons, GCP icons, Kubernetes.

The icon of a diagram element drawn so, in this order: the arch-vis:iconSymbol of its own style (for the
presentation context shown, else for every context); its model element's picture for that context —
its core arch:altVisNotation with that arch:inPresentationContext, else its arch:prefVisNotation; its
notation's arch-vis:iconSymbol.

A model element's picture where its diagram element is drawn as a box (arch-vis:ShapeWithBadge,
arch-vis:ShapeOnly): inside it, as an arch-vis:image of its own would be — at its arch-vis:imagePosition
— unless its style gives it an image or an icon of its own; the badge stays the notation's glyph, which
says the element's type. A style saying arch-vis:useElementPicture makes the picture its badge instead,
in place of the glyph.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:ShapeOnly
    a            arch-vis:RenderingMode ;
    rdfs:label   "Shape Only"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shape Only"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Colored shape with the element label centered, no icon. Used for executive-level views, monochrome printing, or when visual simplicity is preferred.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote '''Algorithm:
1. Draw shape (shapeType) at defaultWidth × defaultHeight
2. Fill with fillColor, stroke with lineColor
3. Center the label (skos:prefLabel) in the shape
4. Do NOT render any icon — ignore iconSymbol and iconPlacement entirely
5. Optionally reduce dimensions for a more compact appearance

Visual layout:
┌──────────────────────┐
│                      │
│    Element Label     │  ← label only, no icon
│                      │
└──────────────────────┘
     filled with fillColor

Used by: Executive dashboards, printed reports, accessibility-focused views.'''@en ;
.


#################################################################
# Line Style and Decoration (for relationships)
#################################################################

arch-vis:LineStyle
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "LineStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of line rendering styles: for relationship connectors (arch-vis:lineStyle) and shape outlines (arch-vis:borderStyle)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:SolidLine
    a            arch-vis:LineStyle ;
    rdfs:label   "Solid Line"@en ;
.

arch-vis:DashedLine
    a            arch-vis:LineStyle ;
    rdfs:label   "Dashed Line"@en ;
.

arch-vis:DottedLine
    a            arch-vis:LineStyle ;
    rdfs:label   "Dotted Line"@en ;
.

arch-vis:Decoration
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "Decoration"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of endpoint decorations for relationship connectors."@en ;
.

arch-vis:decorSymbol
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "decorSymbol"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''URI of the SVG marker symbol for this decoration (typically a small arrow or diamond shape).
The symbol is drawn pointing in the +x direction, towards the endpoint it decorates. The connector meets it at
the middle of the right edge of its viewBox, so a renderer anchors that point on the endpoint and orients the
symbol along the connector; at a source end it mirrors the symbol, so that it points at the source. Strokes and
fills that take the connector's colour are currentColor; a hollow symbol's interior is white. The published
symbols use a 12 x 12 viewBox, drawn at that size in user units.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:NoDecoration
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "No Decoration"@en ;
.

arch-vis:OpenArrowhead
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Open Arrowhead"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Open (unfilled) arrowhead pointing at the endpoint."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/open-arrowhead.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:ClosedArrowhead
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Closed Arrowhead"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled (closed) arrowhead pointing at the endpoint — ArchiMate's triggering, flow and assignment."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/closed-arrowhead.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:HollowArrowhead
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Hollow Arrowhead"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Hollow (white-filled) arrowhead — ArchiMate's realization and specialization."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/hollow-arrowhead.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:HollowDiamond
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Hollow Diamond"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Hollow diamond — used for aggregation."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/hollow-diamond.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:FilledDiamond
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Filled Diamond"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled diamond — used for composition."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/filled-diamond.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:HalfArrowhead
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Half Arrowhead"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Half (one-sided) arrowhead, one barb only."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/half-arrowhead.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.

arch-vis:CircleDot
    a            arch-vis:Decoration ;
    rdfs:label   "Dot"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A small filled circle at the end of the connector — ArchiMate's assignment, at the source end."@en ;
    arch-vis:decorSymbol "https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis/markers/circle-dot.svg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
.


#################################################################
# NotationSet and VisualNotation Classes
#################################################################

arch-vis:NotationSet
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "NotationSet"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''A named collection of visual notation descriptors for a modeling language.
Multiple notation sets allow alternative visual representations (standard, simplified,
monochrome, etc.) for the same metamodel.

A Metamodel links to its available notation sets via arch:notationSet (the default set).
PresentationContext concepts link to their preferred notation set via arch:usesNotationSet,
enabling context-aware palette generation:

  Metamodel ──arch:notationSet──► NotationSet (default)
  PresentationContext ──arch:usesNotationSet──► NotationSet (context-specific)

A palette generator determines the active PresentationContext, follows usesNotationSet
to get the appropriate NotationSet, then queries VisualNotation instances within it.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:VisualNotation
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "VisualNotation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''A structured visual notation descriptor for a single ModelConcept.
Bundles a default Style (shape, color, icon) with the rendered composite image
(arch:prefVisNotation). Used by palette generators and diagram editors to construct
element renderings from primitives.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:notationFor
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "notationFor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a VisualNotation descriptor to the ModelConcept it describes."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:VisualNotation ;
    rdfs:range   arch:ModelConcept ;
.

arch-vis:inNotationSet
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "inNotationSet"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a VisualNotation to the NotationSet it belongs to."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:VisualNotation ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:NotationSet ;
.

arch-vis:LabelExpression
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "LabelExpression"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''What a concept's label says on a diagram, as text with placeholders (arch-vis:expression) —
in one presentation context when it names one (core's arch:inPresentationContext), else in every one.

The text is drawn as it is, but for what stands between braces:
- {name}: the concept's name for the presentation context shown — its name for that audience (a
  skosxl:altLabel with that arch:inPresentationContext), else its skos:prefLabel;
- {type}: the name of its type;
- {prefix:local} or {<iri>}: the values of that property of the concept — a literal as it is (in the
  language shown, or with none), a resource by its name for the context shown — several joined by ", ";
  prefixes are those the vocabularies in use declare; {skos:prefLabel} is the canonical name;
- a path: properties joined by /, each followed from the concept on, ^ before one to follow it backwards
  ({am:assignedTo/skos:prefLabel}, {^arch:target});
- {{ and }}: a brace.
Its lines are the label's lines; a line whose placeholders all come to nothing is left out, so a line
can be there only when the concept has what it shows.'''@en ;
.

arch-vis:labelExpression
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "labelExpression"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''What a concept's label says on a diagram (an arch-vis:LabelExpression), in place of its
name alone: on a diagram element's own style (arch-vis:style), that element's; on a concept's
VisualNotation, every element of that concept's drawn with the notation; on a presentation context (a
concept of a metamodel's arch:presentationContextScheme), every element shown in that context that
nothing else says for. The first that applies wins, in this order:
1. the diagram element's own, for the context shown (arch:inPresentationContext);
2. the diagram element's own, for every context;
3. its concept's notation's, for the context shown;
4. its concept's notation's, for every context;
5. the context's;
with none, the label is the concept's name for the context shown.'''@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes arch-vis:Style, arch-vis:VisualNotation, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:LabelExpression ;
.

arch-vis:expression
    a            owl:DatatypeProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "expression"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A label expression's text, its placeholders between braces (see arch-vis:LabelExpression)."@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:LabelExpression ;
    rdfs:range   xsd:string ;
.

arch-vis:nestedEnd
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "nestedEnd"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''On a relationship's notation: the relationship may be drawn by nesting one end
inside the other (arch-vis:inNode) instead of a line, and this is the end drawn inside. A
composition's part inside its whole is arch-vis:TargetEnd; ArchiMate's specialized element
inside the generic one is arch-vis:SourceEnd; a relationship nesting may draw either way has
both. Absent, nesting does not draw the relationship.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:VisualNotation ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:RelationshipEnd ;
.

arch-vis:RelationshipEnd
    a            owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label   "RelationshipEnd"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumeration of the two ends of a relationship: its source and its target."@en ;
.

arch-vis:SourceEnd
    a            arch-vis:RelationshipEnd ;
    rdfs:label   "SourceEnd"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The end a relationship goes from (arch:source)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:TargetEnd
    a            arch-vis:RelationshipEnd ;
    rdfs:label   "TargetEnd"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The end a relationship goes to (arch:target)."@en ;
.

arch-vis:defaultStyle
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "defaultStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Links a VisualNotation to its default Style — the construction recipe
for rendering this concept. Contains shape type, colors, icon, and dimensions.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:VisualNotation ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Style ;
.

arch-vis:alternativeStyle
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "alternativeStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''Another figure the concept may be drawn as, besides its default Style
(arch-vis:defaultStyle) — a business object, for example, as a box with its glyph or as a banded
box. It says only what differs from the default: its shape type and rendering mode, an outline's
style; colours and the glyph are the default's. A diagram element drawn as it says so on its own style
(arch-vis:style) with the alternative's arch-vis:shapeType and arch-vis:renderingMode.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain  arch-vis:VisualNotation ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Style ;
.

arch-vis:lineStyle
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "lineStyle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The line rendering style (solid, dashed, dotted): of a relationship type's lines, said on its
notation or its notation's default style; or of one line, said on that Link's own style
(arch-vis:style) — a line standing for no relationship, a note's to what it is about, whose look is
its own.'''@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes arch:QualifiedRelationship, arch-vis:VisualNotation, arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:LineStyle ;
.

arch-vis:sourceDecor
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "sourceDecor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The decoration at a line's source end: of a relationship type's lines, said on its notation
or its notation's default style; or of one line, said on that Link's own style, as for
arch-vis:lineStyle.'''@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes arch:QualifiedRelationship, arch-vis:VisualNotation, arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Decoration ;
.

arch-vis:targetDecor
    a            owl:ObjectProperty,
                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label   "targetDecor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment '''The decoration at a line's target end: of a relationship type's lines, said on its notation
or its notation's default style; or of one line, said on that Link's own style, as for
arch-vis:lineStyle.'''@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes arch:QualifiedRelationship, arch-vis:VisualNotation, arch-vis:Style ;
    rdfs:range   arch-vis:Decoration ;
.
